::ENG3_U03_L05_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says the city should widen the main road because carts were delayed for two hours during the festival. Argument B says the city should limit festival carts because carts were delayed for two hours during the festival. What information do both arguments use? {
~The festival was canceled.#The passage says the festival happened.
=Carts were delayed for two hours during the festival.#Correct. Both authors use the same delay detail.
~The city has no roads.#The main road is mentioned.
~Citizens stopped using carts.#The passage does not say that.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says road widening would improve travel. Argument B says limiting carts would reduce crowding. What is Argument A's end? {
~Limit festival carts.#That is Argument B's end.
~Cancel the festival.#Neither argument supports cancellation.
=Widen the main road.#Correct. Argument A uses the delay to support road widening.
~Close the main road.#The argument supports changing the road, not closing it.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says the city should widen the main road because carts were delayed. Argument B says the city should limit festival carts because carts were delayed. How does Argument B use the shared information? {
~To prove roads are unnecessary.#The argument focuses on traffic limits.
~To support building a second festival.#No second festival is proposed.
~To praise road widening.#That is closer to Argument A.
=To argue that cart traffic should be controlled.#Correct. B uses the delay as evidence of crowding caused by too many carts.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says a full courthouse proves strong civic interest. Argument B says a full courthouse proves the room is too small. Which comparison is strongest? {
=Both authors use the full courthouse detail, but A uses it to show civic interest while B uses it to show limited space.#Correct. This identifies the same information and different ends.
~Both authors use different evidence to make the same claim.#They use the same detail for different claims.
~Only Argument A uses evidence.#Both use the full courthouse detail.
~Only Argument B has a claim.#Both arguments make claims.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says a crowded library proves students need more books. Argument B says a crowded library proves students need longer library hours. What shared information is used? {
~The library is empty.#The library is crowded.
~Students dislike books.#The passage does not say this.
=The library is crowded.#Correct. Both arguments begin with the same crowding detail.
~The library is closed forever.#No permanent closure is mentioned.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says a crowded library proves students need more books. Argument B says a crowded library proves students need longer library hours. Which statement explains the different ends? {
~A supports shorter hours, while B supports fewer books.#This reverses and changes the claims.
=A uses crowding to argue for more materials, while B uses crowding to argue for more access time.#Correct. The same information supports different solutions.
~Both arguments end with the same solution.#The solutions differ.
~Neither argument uses the crowding detail.#Both arguments use it.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says the town should repair the theater because every performance sold out. Argument B says the town should build a larger theater because every performance sold out. What is the shared information? {
~The theater had no performances.#The passage says performances sold out.
~The town wants no theater.#Both arguments support theater action.
~The theater is new.#The passage does not say this.
=Every performance sold out.#Correct. This detail appears in both arguments.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says the town should repair the theater because every performance sold out. Argument B says the town should build a larger theater because every performance sold out. Which evaluation is most precise? {
=Argument A treats sellouts as proof the theater is worth preserving, while Argument B treats sellouts as proof the theater needs more capacity.#Correct. This evaluates how the same information is used differently.
~Argument A and B have no shared information.#Both use the sellout detail.
~Argument B proves performances should stop.#B supports building a larger theater.
~Argument A is about roads, while B is about theater.#Both focus on the theater.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says the school should add debate practice because students asked many questions after the assembly. Argument B says the school should shorten assemblies because students asked many questions after the assembly. What different end does Argument B support? {
~Add debate practice.#That is Argument A's end.
=Shorten assemblies.#Correct. B uses the questions as a reason to change assembly length.
~Remove all student questions.#The argument does not say questions should be banned.
~Build a new school.#No building claim is made.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says students asked many questions, proving they need debate practice. Argument B says students asked many questions, proving assemblies need better focus. What is the same information? {
~Students refused to attend.#Attendance is not the shared information.
~Students wrote essays.#No essays are mentioned.
=Students asked many questions.#Correct. Both authors use this detail.
~Teachers canceled debate.#The passage does not say that.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says the river path should be promoted because walkers doubled this spring. Argument B says the river path should be protected from overuse because walkers doubled this spring. How does B use the same information? {
~To prove nobody uses the path.#The number of walkers doubled.
~To argue for closing the river itself.#No such claim appears.
~To support more promotion only.#That is Argument A's direction.
=To argue that increased use may harm the path.#Correct. B uses growth as a reason for protection.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says walkers doubled, so the path should be promoted. Argument B says walkers doubled, so the path should be protected. Which comparison is accurate? {
=A treats growth as an opportunity, while B treats growth as a risk.#Correct. The same fact leads to different ends.
~A and B both argue the path should be destroyed.#Neither says that.
~Only B mentions walkers.#Both mention walkers.
~A uses safety evidence, while B uses tradition evidence.#Those details are not in the passage.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q13::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says the council should publish meeting notes because residents requested copies. Argument B says the council should hold live forums because residents requested copies. What does the shared information show in both arguments? {
~Residents are uninterested.#They requested copies, showing interest.
~The council has no meetings.#Meeting notes are mentioned.
=Residents want more access to public information.#Correct. Both arguments use the requests as evidence of public interest.
~Live forums already happen every day.#The passage does not say this.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q14::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says residents requested copies, so meeting notes should be published. Argument B says residents requested copies, so live forums should be held. What different ends are being supported? {
~Both arguments support canceling public access.#Both support more access.
=Published notes versus live forums.#Correct. The same interest supports two different access methods.
~Private meetings versus secret records.#Neither supports secrecy.
~Longer roads versus wider roads.#Roads are not discussed.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q15::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says the museum should extend hours because evening attendance increased. Argument B says the museum should add timed tickets because evening attendance increased. How does Argument B use the shared information? {
~To argue that attendance is falling.#Attendance increased.
~To show museums should close.#B supports timed tickets, not closure.
~To claim art is unimportant.#No such claim appears.
=To support managing crowds through scheduled entry.#Correct. B uses increased attendance as a crowd-management reason.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q16::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says evening attendance increased, so the museum should extend hours. Argument B says evening attendance increased, so the museum should add timed tickets. Which answer best evaluates the reasoning? {
=Both arguments are valid if increased attendance creates either demand for more time or a need for crowd control.#Correct. This fairly evaluates both uses of the same information.
~Both arguments are invalid because they share a fact.#Sharing a fact does not make reasoning invalid.
~Only Argument A can be valid because it appears first.#Order does not determine validity.
~Only Argument B uses any evidence.#Both use attendance evidence.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q17::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says the city should praise volunteers because they planted fifty trees. Argument B says the city should fund irrigation because volunteers planted fifty trees. What is the shared information? {
~The city cut down fifty trees.#The passage says volunteers planted trees.
~The volunteers refused to help.#They planted trees.
=Volunteers planted fifty trees.#Correct. This detail is used by both authors.
~Irrigation failed last year.#The passage does not say this.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q18::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says volunteers planted fifty trees, so they deserve public praise. Argument B says volunteers planted fifty trees, so the city should fund irrigation. What different end does Argument A support? {
~Fund irrigation.#That is Argument B's end.
=Publicly praise volunteers.#Correct. A uses the planting detail to support recognition.
~Stop planting trees.#Neither argument supports stopping.
~Remove public volunteers.#No such claim appears.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q19::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says the court should simplify forms because many citizens made errors. Argument B says the court should offer legal workshops because many citizens made errors. Which comparison is strongest? {
~A and B use different problems and different evidence.#They use the same error detail.
~A says citizens are careless, while B says forms are perfect.#Neither says that.
~A and B both reject helping citizens.#Both support help.
=Both use citizen errors as evidence, but A supports clearer forms while B supports instruction.#Correct. This identifies shared information and different ends.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q20::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says many citizens made errors on court forms. Argument B uses the same fact to support legal workshops. Why might Argument B be effective? {
=It connects the errors to a need for instruction.#Correct. Workshops directly address lack of understanding.
~It ignores the errors completely.#It uses the errors as evidence.
~It proves forms should be hidden.#No such claim appears.
~It argues that citizens should avoid court.#The passage does not say that.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q21::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says the school garden should expand because students harvested enough vegetables for two meals. Argument B says the school garden should stay small because students harvested enough vegetables for two meals. What does A use the harvest detail to support? {
~Keeping the garden small.#That is Argument B's end.
=Expanding the garden.#Correct. A treats the harvest as proof of success.
~Ending all gardening.#Neither argument supports ending it.
~Replacing vegetables with textbooks.#No such claim appears.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q22::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says the school garden should expand because students harvested enough vegetables for two meals. Argument B says it should stay small because the same harvest was manageable. How does B use the shared information? {
~As proof that students learned nothing.#No learning claim is made.
~As proof the garden failed.#B treats the harvest as manageable, not a failure.
=As evidence that the current size works well.#Correct. B uses the harvest to support keeping the garden small.
~As evidence for a new theater.#Theater is unrelated.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q23::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says the town should add benches because elders rested on walls during the parade. Argument B says the town should improve parade routes because elders rested on walls during the parade. What is the shared information? {
~The parade was canceled.#The passage says a parade happened.
~Elders refused to attend.#They attended and rested.
~The town removed all walls.#The passage says elders rested on walls.
=Elders rested on walls during the parade.#Correct. Both authors use this same detail.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q24::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says elders rested on walls, so the town should add benches. Argument B says elders rested on walls, so the town should improve parade routes. Which evaluation is best? {
=A focuses on seating needs, while B focuses on movement and route design.#Correct. The same observation supports different solutions.
~A and B make the same claim in different words.#The solutions differ.
~B has no reasoning.#B connects the detail to route improvement.
~A proves elders caused the parade problem.#The passage does not blame elders.
}

::ENG3_U03_L05_Q25::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Passage: Argument A says the archive should digitize records because researchers traveled from five counties. Argument B says the archive should improve its reading room because researchers traveled from five counties. Which response best explains the different ends? {
~Both authors argue that researchers should stop traveling.#Neither says that.
~Argument A and B ignore the travel detail.#Both use it.
=A uses researcher travel to support remote access, while B uses it to support better in-person access.#Correct. This explains how the same information supports different ends.
~A supports closing the archive, while B supports deleting records.#Neither claim is made.
}
